Dear Reviewer m7Sg,

Thank you for your thoughtful review and for recognizing that our work "identifies a potentially important and underappreciated threat vector," noting that the high jailbreak susceptibility from random directions is "surprising enough to be important." We are grateful for this positive assessment and for your constructive feedback, which we address below.

&nbsp; 

**(Weakness 1) The claim made in the abstract and introduction that steering with benign SAE features is even more alignment-breaking that steering with random directions appears to be based on a single prompt and model, evaluated at a depth that's not optimal for random directions (Figure 2); the more comprehensive evaluation shown in Figure 3 shows precisely the opposite, with Llama3-8b-it compliance rates much higher with random vectors on every prompt category save one. This claim also appears misleadingly in the Conclusion, where random steering on one model is compared with SAE steering on another.**


We thank the reviewer for this important observation. The comparison was complicated by the use of different steering depths for random vectors (1/3 depth) and SAE features (2/3 depth) in Figure 3. We will revise our claims to more accurately reflect that SAE features demonstrate a comparable potential to random noise. The key finding that semantically benign features offer no safety advantage is a critical and surprising result in itself, and we will frame it as such.

&nbsp; 

**(Weakness 2) The judge model used is quite small, despite the fact that much better models could be used at very modest cost. The authors argue that manual labelings mostly agreed with the model; still, this is an unnecessary source of uncertainty added to critical evaluations.**
We selected Qwen3-8B in thinking mode as our judge model because it provided a strong balance between classification accuracy and computational feasibility. Given the large volume of text to evaluate (3 model families × 1,000 vectors × 100 harmful prompts = 300,000 responses), using a larger judge model was computationally prohibitive for our study. Moreover, our primary goal was not to estimate the exact probability of harmful steering but to demonstrate that it is non-negligible (i.e., to establish a reliable lower bound). The high precision (94%) of our LLM-as-judge, as measured against human evaluation, supports the robustness of this lower-bound estimate (see more details in Appendix B).

&nbsp; 

**(Weakness 3) Figures lack confidence intervals.**


We will add confidence intervals to all compliance rate estimates in the revised paper. We note that Figure 2 already includes standard deviation bars, and we will extend this rigor throughout all figures to ensure statistical clarity.

&nbsp; 

**(Weakness 4) Figure 4b is quite hard to interpret on its own.**


We acknowledge that Figure 4b could be clearer. We will improve its caption and main text description to better explain the cross-category generalization probabilities.

&nbsp; 

**(Weakness 5) The claim that to be used as an attack this method only requires "black-box API access" (line 461) seems misleading, as with the exception of Goodfire's research API designed specifically for this purpose, hosts do not allow steering via API; the practical implication of this work are that researchers should test for jailbreak vulnerability before deploying steering to production.**


This is a fair point.  Our claim about "black-box API access" was meant to contrast our method with attacks that require white-box access to model gradients or internal weights. We agree that this formulation is misleading for a general reader, as standard production APIs do not offer steering capabilities. We will revise to specify this applies to research APIs like Goodfire's, not standard production systems.

&nbsp; 

**(Question 1) Figure 2b shows a Llama3-8b-it 2/3rds depth random steering compliance rate across scaling coefficients that differs from the one shown in Figure 2c; why?**

This discrepancy arises because Figure 2b uses Llama3-8B, while Figure 2c uses Llama3.1-8B. The two models, though similar, have different alignment training and internal representations, leading to small variations in steering sensitivity. We will clarify this distinction in the revised paper to avoid confusion.

&nbsp; 

**(Question 2) How were the 1000 SAE features chosen?**

The 1,000 SAE features were randomly sampled from the full set of features in the Goodfire SAE for Llama3.1-8B layer 19.

&nbsp; 

**(Question 3) What temperature(s) was used?**

We used greedy decoding (temperature = 0) for all experiments to ensure deterministic and reproducible generations.
